<!-- SECTION 1: Introduction -->
<section id="cos-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Cosine Ratio Calculator?</h2>
  <p>A cosine ratio calculator answers one question: for an acute angle in a right triangle, how long is the adjacent side compared with the hypotenuse? Enter the two side lengths, and the tool divides the adjacent side by the hypotenuse, returning <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} />. With an adjacent side of 4 cm and a hypotenuse of 5 cm, the calculation is <span set:html={rich('[[4/5=0.8]]')} />: the adjacent side is 80 percent of the hypotenuse. That decimal is the complete answer. It carries no units, since centimetres cancel during division, and it is not an angle in degrees. The opposite side never enters this calculation. From that single ratio you can compare shapes, check measurements, find horizontal projections, or recover the angle with inverse cosine.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: Understanding the right triangle -->
<section id="cos-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Adjacent Side and Hypotenuse</h2>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle of exactly <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the angle between two perpendicular lines, like a wall meeting a level floor. The hypotenuse sits opposite that right angle and is always the longest side. The other two angles share the remaining 90 degrees, so both are acute. Cosine compares two of those sides in a fixed relationship.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>The adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the leg that touches the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> without being the hypotenuse. It often represents a horizontal run: ground distance from a ladder, a rafter's horizontal span, or a projection of a force. Which leg counts as adjacent depends on the reference angle, so switching from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> swaps the adjacent and opposite labels.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the side opposite the right angle: in any diagram it faces the small square marking the <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> corner, and it is the longest side. In real settings it may be a ladder, a rafter, a ramp, a cable, or a straight-line path, and it is always strictly longer than the adjacent side.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> sits directly across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and never touches its vertex. It plays no role in the cosine formula, which is why the calculator never asks for it. Keep the reference angle straight: pick the wrong angle and the opposite and adjacent labels silently swap.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('cosine-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle with angle A bottom-left, gold adjacent side b, blue hypotenuse c, neutral opposite side a, cos(A)=b/c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Angle A sits at the bottom-left; cosine uses the adjacent side b and the hypotenuse c, not the opposite side a.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: What is cosine -->
<section id="cos-whatis" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Cosine Ratio</h2>
  <p>Cosine compares the leg next to angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> with the complete sloping side:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\cos(A)=\\frac{b}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)]]')} /> is the cosine of angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the adjacent side, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse: the share of the hypotenuse covered by the adjacent side.</p>

  <h3>Understanding SOH-CAH-TOA</h3>
  <p>SOH: Sine = Opposite / Hypotenuse. CAH: Cosine = Adjacent / Hypotenuse. TOA: Tangent = Opposite / Adjacent. The letters CAH spell out the cosine formula: the first letter names the function, the second the numerator, and the third the denominator.</p>

  <h3>Why Cosine Is a Ratio</h3>
  <p>Dividing two lengths cancels the units: <span set:html={rich('[[4\\text{ m}/5\\text{ m}=0.8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[400\\text{ cm}/500\\text{ cm}=0.8]]')} /> as well. The same physical sides always yield the same number, whatever unit you measured them in, as long as both share it.</p>

  <h3>What Does a Cosine Value of 0.8 Mean?</h3>
  <p>It means the adjacent side is 80 percent of the hypotenuse: a long leg next to the angle, a hypotenuse only slightly longer. It does not give the angle directly; that needs inverse cosine.</p>

  <h3>Why Cosine Is Less Than 1</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is longer than either leg, so <span set:html={rich('[[b \\lt c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b/c \\lt 1]]')} />, and both sides are positive, so the ratio stays above 0. For a valid, non-degenerate right triangle, cosine always lands strictly between 0 and 1; the boundary values 0 and 1 are limits, not reachable values.</p>

  <h3>Cosine Versus Sine</h3>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=a/c]]')} /> share the hypotenuse but use different legs. Choose cosine when the adjacent side is known or needed; choose sine when the opposite side is. Confusing the two is the most common early error.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('cosine-ratio.svg')} alt="Three stages: 3-4-5 triangle labeled, cosine formula cos(A)=b/c, result 4/5=0.8, the adjacent side is 80 percent" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Three stages: identify the sides, apply cos(A) = b/c, and divide. The adjacent side is 80 percent of the hypotenuse.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 4: Formula derivation -->
<section id="cos-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Cosine Formula Works</h2>
  <p>Start from the definition: cosine is the adjacent side divided by the hypotenuse. In this page's notation:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=\\frac{b}{c}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The adjacent side is the numerator because it is the leg being compared; the hypotenuse is the denominator because it is the reference length.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p>Step 1: Write the formula. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2: Substitute the measurements. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3: Divide. <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.8}]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 4: Interpret the answer. The adjacent side is 80 percent of the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>Step 5: Verify the triangle. <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[9+16=25]]')} />. The side lengths are mathematically consistent.</p>
  <p>If you want the angle rather than the ratio, inverse cosine is the next step: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(0.8)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. That is a separate calculation; this page's tool returns the ratio only.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 5: Calculator walkthrough -->
<section id="cos-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Cosine Ratio Calculator</h2>
  <p>The walkthrough below matches the actual interface on this page.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the reference angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />,</strong> since the adjacent side is defined relative to it.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />:</strong> the leg touching angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> that is not the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />:</strong> the side opposite the right angle, the longest side.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure or obtain both side lengths</strong> in the same unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the adjacent-side length</strong> (the smaller value) in the first field.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the hypotenuse</strong> (the larger value) in the second field.</li>
    <li><strong>Select matching units with the unit selectors</strong> so the division is meaningful.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm both values are positive;</strong> zero or negative lengths cannot form a triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that the hypotenuse is longer than the adjacent side;</strong> if not, the inputs are swapped or mislabeled.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate cos(A)</strong> to divide the adjacent side by the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the cosine ratio:</strong> the adjacent side as a share of the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify the answer is between 0 and 1;</strong> anything outside that range signals an input error.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset</strong> to clear the inputs and start over.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The calculator never asks for an angle in degrees, the opposite side, or a value to invert: two side lengths are the complete input.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 6: Four foundational examples -->
<section id="cos-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Calculating Cosine</h2>
  <p>Each example follows the same pattern: write the formula, substitute, divide.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: A 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ cm}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.8}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The adjacent side is 80 percent of the hypotenuse; the opposite side completes the triangle but the formula ignores it.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: A 5-12-13 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\text{ m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=12/13]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)\\approx0.9231}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The adjacent side is about 92.31 percent of the hypotenuse; the 5 m opposite side is not needed.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: A 30-60-90 Triangle</h3>
  <p>For angle <span set:html={rich('[[A=30^\\circ]]')} />, the adjacent side is <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{3}/2]]')} /> times the hypotenuse. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{3}\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=2\\text{ cm}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(30^\\circ)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(30^\\circ)\\approx0.8660}]]')} /></p>
  <p>This special-angle value is worth memorizing; the 30-60-90 triangle appears constantly in geometry problems.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: A Triangle With an Adjacent Side of 8 and Hypotenuse of 10</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=8\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=8/10]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.8}]]')} /></p>
  <p>The ratio matches the 3-4-5 triangle because scaling all sides by the same factor leaves the angles and ratios unchanged.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 7: Twelve practical examples -->
<section id="cos-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Examples of Using the Cosine Ratio</h2>
  <p>Cosine is a mathematical ratio, not a physical quantity with units. Its practical value lies in comparing the adjacent side with the hypotenuse, computing horizontal projections, and verifying measurements. Each example assumes a simplified right triangle.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Calculating a Ladder's Cosine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A 5 m ladder stands with its base 3 m from the wall; angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> lies between the ladder and the level ground. The ground distance is the adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b=3\\text{ m}]]')} />, the ladder the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=3/5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.6}]]')} />: the ground distance is 60 percent of the ladder's length. The ratio alone does not establish ladder safety.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Calculating a Roof Rafter's Cosine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A roof rafter slopes 5 m with a horizontal run of 4 m: the run is the adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />, the rafter the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.8}]]')} />: the run is 80 percent of the sloping length. A geometric ratio, not a structural assessment.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Evaluating a Ramp's Cosine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A ramp slopes 9.5 m with a horizontal run of 9 m: <span set:html={rich('[[b=9\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=9.5\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=9/9.5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)\\approx0.9474}]]')} />: the run is about 94.74 percent of the sloping length, as expected for a gentle slope. It does not establish accessibility compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: A Surveying Measurement</h3>
  <p>A simplified surveying triangle records a 50 m straight-line distance and a 40 m horizontal separation: <span set:html={rich('[[b=40\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=50\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=40/50]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.8}]]')} />: the horizontal distance is 80 percent of the straight-line distance. Real surveying may need instrument-height corrections.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Finding a Guy Wire's Cosine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A 13 m guy wire anchors 12 m horizontally from the pole: <span set:html={rich('[[b=12\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=12/13]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)\\approx0.9231}]]')} />: the separation is about 92.31 percent of the wire's length. It says nothing about wire tension or anchor strength.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Calculating a Staircase Stringer's Cosine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A simplified staircase stringer is 5 m long with a 4 m horizontal run: <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.8}]]')} />: the run is 80 percent of the diagonal stringer. It does not verify code compliance.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Understanding a Crane Boom's Horizontal Projection</h3>
  <p>A simplified crane boom is 10 m long with a 6 m horizontal projection: <span set:html={rich('[[b=6\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=6/10]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.6}]]')} />: the projection is 60 percent of the boom's length. It does not establish lifting capacity.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: A Straight Aircraft Flight Segment</h3>
  <p>An aircraft travels 5 km along a straight path, covering 4 km horizontally: <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ km}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ km}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.8}]]')} />: the horizontal displacement is 80 percent of the path length. An idealized model, not flight navigation.</p>

  <h3>Example 13: Calculating a Diagonal Brace's Cosine Ratio</h3>
  <p>A diagonal brace is 2 m long with a 1.2 m horizontal projection: <span set:html={rich('[[b=1.2\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=2\\text{ m}]]')} />. <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=1.2/2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{\\cos(A)=0.6}]]')} />: the projection is 60 percent of the brace's length. It does not determine brace strength.</p>

  <h3>Example 14: Calculating a Hiking Trail's Cosine Ratio</h3>
