<!-- SECTION 8: Cosine and angle size -->
<section id="cos-angle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Relationship Between Cosine and Angle Size</h2>
  <p>Cosine shrinks as the acute angle grows. A small angle keeps the adjacent side nearly as long as the hypotenuse, so the ratio sits close to 1; as angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> opens toward <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} />, the adjacent side becomes a smaller share and the ratio falls. The verified reference values below show the pattern:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Angle A</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Adjacent side b</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Hypotenuse c</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">Cosine ratio</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">30 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sqrt(3)</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">2</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 0.8660</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">45 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sqrt(2)</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 0.7071</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">60 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">2</td><td class="py-2.5">0.5</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">80 deg</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Approximately 5.7588</td><td class="py-2.5">Approximately 0.1736</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The table measurements are illustrative: the ratios, not the absolute lengths, determine the angles. The ratio approaches 0 as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ]]')} /> and approaches 1 as the angle approaches <span set:html={rich('[[0^\\circ]]')} />, staying strictly between for every valid triangle.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('cosine-steps.svg')} alt="Three triangles at 30, 45, 60 degrees with cosine values 0.8660, 0.7071, 0.5; cosine falls as angle grows" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">As angle A grows, the adjacent side becomes a smaller share of the hypotenuse, so cos(A) falls.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>The calculator takes no angle input; these reference values serve as orientation and check values when verifying a result.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 9: Reverse formulas -->
<section id="cos-recover" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Finding Sides and Angles With Cosine</h2>
  <p>The cosine formula rearranges cleanly to recover any one of its three quantities when the other two are known.</p>
  <h3>How to Find the Adjacent Side</h3>
  <p>Start with <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} /> and multiply both sides by <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{b=c\\cos(A)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A=60^\\circ]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[b=10\\cos(60^\\circ)]]')} />, and since <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(60^\\circ)=0.5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{b=5\\text{ m}}]]')} />: half the hypotenuse for this angle.</p>
  <h3>How to Find the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Solving for <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> instead gives:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{c=\\frac{b}{\\cos(A)}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[A=60^\\circ]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[c=4/0.5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{c=8\\text{ m}}]]')} /></p>
  <h3>How to Find an Angle From a Cosine Ratio</h3>
  <p>The cosine ratio and the angle are different quantities. If the ratio is known, inverse cosine, also called arccosine, recovers the angle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A=\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{b}{c}\\right)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=0.8]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(0.8)]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[\\boxed{A\\approx36.87^\\circ}]]')} />; set the scientific calculator to degrees mode first. These are mathematical rearrangements: the tool on this page returns the ratio itself.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 10: Common mistakes -->
<section id="cos-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Cosine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Using the opposite side instead of the adjacent side.</strong> This computes <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} />, which is sine. Fix: pick the leg that touches angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Reversing the fraction to <span set:html={rich('[[c/b]]')} />.</strong> The answer exceeds 1. Fix: the longer side must be the denominator.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing cosine with sine.</strong> Reporting 0.6 for a 3-4-5 triangle. Fix: sine uses the opposite side; cosine uses the adjacent side.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing cosine with secant.</strong> Computing <span set:html={rich('[[c/b]]')} />. Fix: secant is the reciprocal of cosine, always above 1.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering an adjacent side longer than the hypotenuse.</strong> The inputs describe an impossible triangle. Fix: the hypotenuse field must hold the longer measurement.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering zero or negative side lengths.</strong> Division by zero or a meaningless negative ratio. Fix: use positive lengths only.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing measurement units.</strong> Centimetres divided by metres skew the ratio by 100. Fix: convert both sides to one unit first.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting cosine as an angle in degrees.</strong> Writing 0.8 degrees. Fix: the ratio is a pure number, not an angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming the cosine ratio has units.</strong> The units already cancelled in the division. Fix: drop the unit from the answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing the reference angle with the other acute angle.</strong> The adjacent and opposite labels swap. Fix: mark angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> on the diagram first.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding too early.</strong> Rounding <span set:html={rich('[[12/13]]')} /> to 0.92 before multiplying back adds avoidable error. Fix: keep extra digits until the final step.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming cosine is the same for every triangle.</strong> Applying one value to a different shape. Fix: cosine depends on the triangle's proportions.</li>
    <li><strong>Thinking the ratio directly gives the angle in degrees.</strong> Reading 0.8 as an angle. Fix: use inverse cosine to recover degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Reading a cosine of 0.8 as 0.8 degrees.</strong> Confusing a ratio with an angle. Fix: 0.8 is the ratio; the angle is about 36.87 degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Naming a leg before identifying the reference angle.</strong> Adjacent and opposite get mixed up. Fix: fix angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> before naming any leg.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />:</p>
  <p>Incorrect: <span set:html={rich('[[5/4=1.25]]')} />. This is the reciprocal of cosine, called secant, and it is not the cosine ratio.</p>
  <p>Correct: <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=4/5=0.8]]')} /></p>
  <p>A valid cosine of an acute angle always sits between 0 and 1.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 11: Verification -->
<section id="cos-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Cosine Result Is Valid</h2>
  <p>A valid, non-degenerate right triangle requires <span set:html={rich('[[0 \\lt b \\lt c]]')} />, and therefore <span set:html={rich('[[0 \\lt b/c \\lt 1]]')} />. Both measurements must use compatible units, and the cosine result must be greater than 0 and less than 1. A result of 1 or more usually indicates reversed inputs, incorrect side identification, or incompatible measurements.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Check the Ratio</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=b/c]]')} /> directly. With <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[4/5=0.8]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Method 2: Check the Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>If the opposite side is known or needed, calculate <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{c^2-b^2}]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[a=\\sqrt{25-16}=3]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=5^2]]')} />. This confirms the dimensions of the 3-4-5 right triangle.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Check Using Inverse Cosine</h3>
  <p>If the ratio is 0.8: <span set:html={rich('[[A=\\cos^{-1}(0.8)\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Then verify that the angle and side ratio are consistent. Minor differences can occur due to rounding.</p>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 12: Choosing a ratio -->
<section id="cos-vs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Cosine vs. Sine, Tangent, and Secant</h2>
  <p>Each ratio compares a different pair of sides, using the same reference angle and side labels:</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/30">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Ratio</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-on-surface">Formula</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-on-surface">What it compares</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-on-surface-variant">
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Sine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sin(A) = a/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosine</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cos(A) = b/c</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to hypotenuse</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Tangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">tan(A) = a/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Opposite side to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Secant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">sec(A) = c/b</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to adjacent side</td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-outline-variant/15"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cosecant</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">csc(A) = c/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Hypotenuse to opposite side</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Cotangent</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">cot(A) = b/a</td><td class="py-2.5">Adjacent side to opposite side</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Secant is the reciprocal of cosine: <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1/\\cos(A)]]')} />. If <span set:html={rich('[[\\cos(A)=0.8]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[\\sec(A)=1/0.8=1.25]]')} />. Use cosine for the adjacent side or a horizontal projection, sine for vertical components, tangent when no hypotenuse is involved, and secant when a formula calls for the reciprocal.</p>
  <p>For the neighbouring ratios, see the <a href="/sine-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Sine Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/tangent-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Tangent Ratio Calculator</a>, the <a href="/secant-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Secant Ratio Calculator</a>, and the <a href="/cosecant-ratio-calculator/" class="text-primary hover:underline">Cosecant Ratio Calculator</a>.</p>
</section>

